From e28739c827d274e07b191ef2175aade400ea5a55 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: shota <monyuonyu@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 16:19:31 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 240/402] Unified: snap parts together and move them point to
 point, with clicks only
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Putting a part against another one needed the Placement dialog, attachment
or assembly joints. Four tools now do it with clicks, with the part moving
right away (preview), Esc/Cancel putting it back and OK one undo step:

- Snap together (くっつける, J): a face of the part to move, then a face of
  the other part: the faces touch, facing each other. Options: flip (same
  direction, e.g. top faces flush), gap, turn in 90 degree steps or any
  angle, and which points go together (the clicked points, the centers, or
  keep the position along the face).
- Line up the axes (軸をそろえる): round faces or circles on one axis (a
  shaft into a hole).
- Point onto a face (点を面に合わせる): a point straight onto a face.
- Move point to point (点から点へ動かす, Shift+M): snaps to vertices, middles
  of edges, centers of circles and faces, with markers and a see-through
  copy following the cursor; Ctrl or a check box for a copy.

Two picks made with Ctrl put the matching tool on the context bar; the
first pick moves onto the second, the panel says which moves where and can
swap them. What moves is the part the element belongs to (a Body, a shape,
a link), or a container around it, chosen in the panel; nothing inside a
link. Everything is computed in global coordinates, so nested App::Part
and rotated parents work. An attached part keeps its attachment with a new
offset, or is detached, as chosen in the panel.

The geometry is in SnapMath.py, tested headless: FreeCADCmd -t TestSnapMath.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
